<div class="lead">
  <p>PDF files are incredibly useful for sharing documents that look the same on any device. But they can get <strong>very large</strong>, making them difficult to email, upload, or share online.</p>
  <p>In this guide, I'll show you exactly how to compress PDFs without losing quality, using our free online tool and some smart techniques.</p>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>Why PDF Files Get So Big</h2>
  <p>Before we dive into compression, it's helpful to understand what makes PDFs large in the first place:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>High-resolution images:</strong> Scanned documents or photos embedded in PDFs can take up massive amounts of space</li>
    <li><strong>Embedded fonts:</strong> PDFs often include entire font files, even if you only use a few characters</li>
    <li><strong>Metadata:</strong> Hidden data like author information, revision history, and thumbnails add bulk</li>
    <li><strong>Multiple pages:</strong> A 100-page document will obviously be larger than a 1-page flyer</li>
  </ul>
</section>

