<section>
  <h2>Pro Tips for Better PDF Compression</h2>

  <h3>1. Choose the Right Compression Level</h3>
  <p>Not all PDFs need the same treatment:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Text-only documents → Use "Maximum" compression (you won't notice any difference)</li>
    <li>Documents with charts/graphs → Use "Balanced" (preserves detail while reducing size)</li>
    <li>Print-ready files → Use "High Quality" (minimal compression, maximum clarity)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Remove Unnecessary Pages First</h3>
  <p>If your PDF has blank pages, cover pages you don't need, or sections nobody reads, remove them before compressing. Use our <a href="/en/tools/split-pdf/">Split PDF tool</a> to extract only what you need.</p>

  <h3>3. Compress Images Separately</h3>
  <p>For scanned documents with lots of images, consider converting images to lower resolution first, then creating your PDF. Our <a href="/en/tools/jpg-to-pdf/">JPG to PDF converter</a> handles this nicely.</p>
</section>

